The converse may not be the case, i.e., if $K$ is a simplicial object of $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C}, X) = \text{SR}(\mathcal{C}/X)$ which defines a hypercovering for the site $\mathcal{C}/X$ as in Hypercoverings, Definition <a href="/tag/09VU" data-tag="09VU">25.6.1</a>, then it may not be true that $K$ defines a hypercovering of $X$. For example, if $K_0 = \{ U_{0, i}\} _{i \in I_0}$ then the latter condition guarantees $\{ U_{0, i} \to X\} $ is a covering of $\mathcal{C}$ whereas the former condition only requires $\coprod h_{U_{0, i}}^\#  \to h_ X^\# $ to be a surjective map of sheaves.